@@ -69,6 +69,27 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Rich can pretty print a JSON string with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json`. The output is re-serialized as multi-line JSON with indentation (4 spaces by default) and is syntax highlighted unless ``highlight=False`` is given::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The JSON renderable disables wrapping on its text. Default printing still crops lines wider than the console width; pass ``soft_wrap=True`` to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` if you need long lines kept whole.
+
+You can also print or log a JSON renderable directly, by importing :class:`~rich.json.JSON`::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.print(JSON('{"name": "rich"}'))
+
+The package-level :func:`rich.print_json` function prints to the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('{"name": "rich"}')
+
+From the command line, a JSON file can be pretty printed with ``python -m rich.json PATH``.
+
